      explanation: "**SAT Pattern: No-Solution Condition**\n\n**Choice C is correct.**\n\n**The Fast Way (~20s):** The listed outputs rise by $5$ for each step of $1$ in $x$, so $f$ has slope $5$ and $f(x) = 5x - 7$. Two lines never meet only when the slopes match and the equations differ, and $y = 5x + 6$ is the one such choice.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\nStep 1: Find the slope of $f$ from the table: $3 - (-2) = 5$, $8 - 3 = 5$, and $13 - 8 = 5$, so the slope is $5$. Using the pair $(1, -2)$: $-2 = 5(1) + b$, so $b = -7$ and $f(x) = 5x - 7$.\nStep 2: A line never intersects $y = f(x)$ exactly when the system of the two equations has no solution — that is, when the two lines are parallel but not identical. Parallel requires slope $5$; not identical requires a $y$-intercept other than $-7$.\nStep 3: Only $y = 5x + 6$ has slope $5$ with a different intercept. Check: $5x - 7 = 5x + 6$ gives $-7 = 6$, which is false for every $x$, so the graphs never meet ✓\n\n**Why the wrong answers are tempting:**\n* Choice A ($y = -5x - 7$): it copies the intercept $-7$, which feels like a match. But the slope is $-5$, so setting $5x - 7 = -5x - 7$ gives $x = 0$ — the graphs meet at $(0, -7)$.\n* Choice B ($y = 5x - 7$): this is $f$ itself. Identical equations intersect at infinitely many points, the opposite of never meeting.\n* Choice D ($y = 6x - 7$): a near-match on slope. Setting $5x - 7 = 6x - 7$ gives $x = 0$, so the lines cross once.\n\n**Test Day Takeaway:** \"Never intersects\" means the system has no solution: equal slopes, different $y$-intercepts. Equal slopes with the same intercept is the other extreme — infinitely many solutions.",
